.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1202 AM PDT Tue Oct 27 2026
(Today and tonight)

Satellite imagery reveals some patchy stratus developing mainly over
the higher elevations of the Bay Area and Central Coast, but also
extending to a patch over Monterey Bay itself. Stratus development
overnight remains somewhat uncertain but some expansion is expected
through the morning hours with stratus dissipating through the late
morning and early afternoon hours.

The weather pattern continues to be dominated by a large scale ridge
with an axis over the Pacific Northwest, as an embedded low
reinforces the larger pattern and brings the troughing influence
farther south into northern and central California. Highs remain
relatively cool for this time of year with middle to upper 60s
expected in the interior valleys, the lower 70s in the warmest parts
of extreme southern Monterey County, lower to middle 60s close to
the Bays, and the upper 50s to lower 60s along the Pacific coast.
Gentle to moderate onshore winds will develop this afternoon with
gusts reaching 20 to 30 mph along the coast, through favored gaps
and passes, and at the ridgelines.

A combination of high astronomical tides and surge from thermal
expansion effects brings the risk of minor coastal flooding along
the Bayshore at the San Pablo, San Francisco, and Monterey Bays, and
the Pacific coast, at high tide today. At the San Francisco tidal
gauge, high tide is expected to be 2.1 ft above normal (7.9 ft MLLW)
at 12:15 PM Tuesday 10/27, including around 1.2 ft of surge residual
partially attributed to impacts from El Nino.

