.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1202 AM PDT Tue Oct 27 2026
(Wednesday through Monday)

Troughing will continue to impact the region through the work week.
The trough pattern will get reinforced through Wednesday as a cut-
off low forming over British Columbia migrates southwards, allowing
a low pressure system flowing in from the central Pacific to merge
into a trough axis that will extend down the West Coast and persist
into the upcoming weekend. The net result will be a continued
gradual cooling trend across the region with highs dipping into
the middle 50s to middle 60s across the lower elevations by the
latter part of the week.

For the Halloween weekend, confidence for significant rainfall has
decreased since yesterday, especially across the SF Bay Area, where
probabilities for rainfall have decreased to around 10-30% and
measurable rainfall chances have all but vanished. The greatest
chances for measurable rainfall remain around Monterey and San
Benito Counties, and there rain totals are expected to be light with
the probability of wetting rains (rain totals above 0.1") rather low
(below 15%). Remember that last night, NBM guidance at the Monterey
Airport suggested that for the 48 hour period beginning 5 AM PDT
Saturday the 31st and ending 4 AM PST Monday the 2nd (note the time
change, as this period includes the "fall back" from Daylight
Savings Time) suggested that the 90th percentile rainfall total (a
reasonable worst case scenario, with a 10% probability that the
final total exceeds this value) was 1.6 inches. Now, for the same
period, that 90th percentile value has dropped to 0.3 inches. It
looks like the model trend is leaning more and more towards the bulk
of the rainfall impacting southern California, and even there the
forecast remains low confidence. An active weather pattern remains
possible through the early part of next week, but any details are
hard to pin down and ensemble model cluster analysis continues to
depict a variable solution space that`s mainly focused around how
fast the troughing and ridging come through the western United
States.

Coastal flooding concerns continue across Bayshore locations within
the San Pablo, San Francisco, and Monterey Bays and the Pacific
coastal regions through Friday. At the San Francisco tidal gauge,
high tide is expected to be 1.9 ft above normal (7.7 ft MLLW) at
12:56 PM  Wednesday 10/28, 1.7 ft above normal (7.5 ft MLLW) at
01:43 PM  Thursday 10/29, 1.6 ft above normal (7.4 ft MLLW) at 02:39
 PM Friday 10/30, and 1.0 feet above normal (6.8 ft MLLW) at 03:44
PM on Saturday 10/31, including around 1.2 ft of surge residual
partially attributed to impacts from El Nino.

