.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 1114 AM PDT Tue Oct 27 2026

VFR conditions are expected for the TAF cycle, with the marine layer
essentially being non-existant. The main challenges will be any
cumulus or stratocu that develop, similar to what we are seeing from
roughly the East Bay and San Mateo County into the northern portion
of the Central Coast. Sites that are seeing cigs from these clouds
are reporting values between FEW or SCT025-035, perhaps some rogue
BKN at times. Any of those BKN025 or BKN030 cigs should be short
lived or temporary. The only other forecast challenge for tonight,
is if KSTS gets any radiational fog/stratus again. Opted to hint at
it with what has happened the past two mornings, but confidence was
too low to lock in LIFR conditions.

Vicinity of SFO...Some cumulus is popping up around SF Bay and
generally appears to be tied to the terrain. Expecting VFR conditions
to prevail through the period, with the onshore winds picking up t
his afternoon. Sustained speeds should be around 12-15kt during the
peak, with gusts up to 20-25kt possible. Winds begin to ease later
in the evening, but will become breezy again mid to late Wednesday
afternoon.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...Satellite imagery is most interesting over
the northern portion of the Central coast where we have a mix of
stratus and stratocu being observed. TAF sites remain VFR here and
are reporting mainly OVC cigs around 035-040. Models aren`t really
depicting this too well and with stratus kind of back filling from
the northwest, current thinking is we should remain cloudy through
the afternoon. If the clouds hold, we could still see BKN VFR cigs
into the early evening. Offshore flow returns to these terminals
tonight, which should clear out any lingering cloud should they
still exist.

